            "id": 104072,
            "time": "2026-11-03T17:41:03Z",
            "is_private": false,
            "creation_time": "2026-11-03T17:41:03Z",
            "creator": "kevinushey",
            "bug_id": 19278,
            "text": "deparse() does not parenthesize a negative numeric constant when it is the left operand of ^ (likewise [, [[ and $), so the deparsed text re-parses as a different expression. Any computed call with a negative constant in that position is affected, e.g. bquote(.(x)^2) with x <- -2, substitute(x^2, list(x = -2)), or call(\"^\", -2, 2): all deparse as \"-2^2\", which parses as -(2^2). Integer and infinite constants (-2L, -Inf) behave the same.\n\nReproducible example: attached as deparse-negative-base-repro.R; eval(e) gives 4 but the re-parsed deparse(e) gives -4, expected 4 (i.e. \"(-2)^2\").\n\nAnalysis:\n\nneedsparens() in src/main/deparse.c has parenthesized complex scalars (which deparse as the binary expression 0-1i) since the 2002 deparse rewrite, but never negative real or integer scalars, which deparse with a unary minus and so bind like one. Seen on R-devel r90833; the code is unchanged in all released versions.\n\nProposed patch: attached as deparse-negative-base.diff. It adds the missing branch, mirroring the existing rule for unary operators: parentheses are added only on the left of a tighter-binding operator, so \"2^-2\", \"-1 * 2\", \"-1:2\" and \"--1\" are unchanged. A regression test is included; make test-Reg passes.\n\nThe patch is also submitted as https://github.com/r-devel/r-svn/pull/452, where CI results can be seen.",
